{"acceleration_structures":[],"air_sha256":"86991f183a6b82ec4a7504ac3d04e26ab9b5e15749c311eac13d1fb9a46d4490","argument_buffer_buffers":[],"argument_buffer_intersection_function_tables":[],"argument_buffer_textures":[],"authored_by":"claude:opus-5","buffers":[],"case_id":"3c92eb7051ae8de33f2065919f6e96cd5cdb6cf0a921082a8a8193189e912f80","compare":{"kind":"exact"},"device_buffer_arrays":[],"dispatch":{"grid":[2,1,1],"threads_per_threadgroup":[1,1,1]},"entry":"CC_ColorizeFloatCoordinates","execution_safety":"loop_free","function_constants":[],"intersection_function_tables":[],"kernel_stage_inputs":[],"name":"fast-cos-flushes-past-the-last-quadrant","output":{"binding":3,"dimensions":[2,1,1],"kind":"texture","origin":[0,0,0]},"rationale":"The entry evaluates fast_cos(input.rg * pi/100 - pi) and maps it to (cos + 1) * 0.5. Texel 0 carries 3e8 and a quiet NaN, whose arguments are 9424775.0 and NaN: both are past the 6588397.5 quadrant boundary where Metal's fast::cos gives up and answers exactly 0.0, so both channels must read back 0.5. Texel 1 carries 100.0 twice, whose argument is exactly 0.0, so both channels must read back 1.0. Every observed value is either the flush constant or cos(0), so the case pins the flush boundary and the NaN arm without depending on any implementation's transcendental precision.","render_targets":[],"samplers":[],"stage":"kernel","texture_arrays":[],"textures":[{"binding":1,"bytes_b64":"GA2PTQAAwH8AAAAAAAAAAAAAyEIAAMhCAAAAAAAAAAA=","dimensions":[2,1,1],"format":"rgba32_float","role":"input","texture_type":"d2"},{"binding":3,"dimensions":[2,1,1],"format":"rgba32_float","initial_bytes_b64":"q6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6s=","role":"output","texture_type":"d2"}],"threadgroup_memory":[],"vertex_inputs":[],"visible_function_references":[],"visible_function_tables":[]}
{"acceleration_structures":[],"air_sha256":"86cd8a81f1927d719dc2659fb2533119f5d5f9345383e0ee5bb6aea48da7eba7","argument_buffer_buffers":[],"argument_buffer_intersection_function_tables":[],"argument_buffer_textures":[],"authored_by":"claude:opus-5","buffers":[{"binding":0,"initial_bytes_b64":"","role":"output"}],"case_id":"9684f817b7e8ac821f6201ee9695802fc8a887efca3d87df87c47dae7c13f4bd","compare":{"kind":"exact"},"device_buffer_arrays":[],"dispatch":{"grid":[32,1,1],"threads_per_threadgroup":[32,1,1]},"entry":"kernel_simd_quad_narrow","execution_safety":"loop_free","function_constants":[],"intersection_function_tables":[],"kernel_stage_inputs":[],"name":"narrow-shuffles-and-quad-reductions-in-one-simdgroup","output":{"binding":0,"kind":"buffer","length":2180,"offset":0},"rationale":"One 32-lane simdgroup reading itself through eight families the corpus calls but no case covered: simd_shuffle_down at half4 and at the three narrow integer widths (uchar, char, short), quad_sum at float3 and float2, and quad_shuffle_down at float3 and float2. The narrow integer slots pin the widths a lowering could silently promote: the char lane carries a negative value so a zero-extending shuffle is caught, and the short lane spans -2900..4600 so a truncation to a byte is too. The quad_sum lanes are reductions over four lanes, not shuffles, so they also pin that the reduction clusters to the quad and not to the simdgroup: every lane of a quad reads back its own quad's sum, and the four quad sums differ. Slots whose source lane leaves the group are overwritten with zero under a branch on tid -- Metal leaves those undefined -- so no lane asserts on undefined behaviour. The 545th word is left at the 0xABABABAB fill and read back as an overrun guard.","render_targets":[],"samplers":[],"stage":"kernel","texture_arrays":[],"textures":[],"threadgroup_memory":[],"vertex_inputs":[],"visible_function_references":[],"visible_function_tables":[]}
